From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: sunxi: stop the card before tearing the data engine down

A failed data transfer is finished in two steps here, and they are in the wrong
order. sunxi_mmc_finalize_request() disables the DMA path, resets the DMA
engine and the FIFO in hard IRQ; only afterwards does
sunxi_mmc_handle_manual_stop() send CMD12 with SMHC_CMD's STOP_ABT_CMD. The
card is still sending in between, and the stop then reports failure.

THE FACT
--------
Measured on the 8 GiB board, 2026-09-19, kernel 6.18.36-t0137, eMMC
SDINBDV4-32G in HS400 200 MHz 8 bit, with a 512 MiB O_DIRECT read at
hs400_ds_dl = 21 -- a data-strobe delay inside the window the driver's own init
sweep accepts, so the transfer errors but the card stays alive. The read never
returns; the task sits in D state, and the only way out is a power cycle.
Register state with the transfer wedged:

    RINTSTS 0x38 = 0x00000020   only RX_DATA_REQUEST -- no error bit standing
    IMASK   0x30 = 0x0000BBC6   the mask for a command WITHOUT data
    STATUS  0x3C = 0x01CE9503   FSM_BUSY=1, FIFO 235 of 256
    CMDR    0x18 = 0x0000014D   START clear, opcode 13
    TBC0    0x48 = 0x00021400   TBC1 0x4C = 0x00020FC4   difference 1084

and in the log, in this order:

    sunxi-mmc 4022000.mmc: data error, sending stop command
    sunxi-mmc 4022000.mmc: send stop command failed

Three things are in that capture. The strayed CMD18 has already been
finalized -- IMASK is the no-data mask and host->mrq points at a later CMD13.
The card is one FIFO ahead of the DMA: TBC0 - TBC1 = 1084 = FIFO level x 4.
And FSM_BUSY is standing with nothing left to service it, which is why
everything issued afterwards waits for ever. The capture was taken on
2026-09-19; the symptom is the permanently hung read described above.

WHAT THE UM SAYS
----------------
a733-um-v0.92, 11.3.8.1, SMHC_CTRL at offset 0x0000:

  [5] DMA_ENB   R/W 0x0  global DMA enable: 0 moves data over the AHB bus
                          instead, 1 uses DMA
  [1] FIFO_RST  R/W 0x0  FIFO reset, clears itself when done
  [0] SOFT_RST  R/W 0x0  software reset of the controller (1 = reset),
                          clears itself when done

DMA_ENB = 0 does not end a transfer -- it re-routes the data path to the AHB
bus, and this driver has no PIO path that would read it there, while
SMHC_FIFO_AC_MOD[31] still says DMA. So the teardown leaves the card sending
into a FIFO that nothing drains and that has just been emptied for it.

11.3.8.7, SMHC_CMD bit 14 STOP_ABT_CMD: marks the command as a stop or abort
of the data transfer in progress. That is the one mechanism the controller
offers for ending the data phase, and it is issued after the data path has
already been taken apart.

SOFT_RST is the only thing that returns the state machines to idle, and this
driver writes it exactly once, in sunxi_mmc_init_host(). The error path resets
the DMA engine and the FIFO and never the controller, so a stop that does not
take leaves FSM_BUSY standing with no way back.

THE THREE DEFECTS
-----------------
1. Order. The data engine is released before the card is stopped
   (sunxi_mmc_finalize_request(), hard IRQ) instead of after it.

2. The stop is judged by events that are not its own. The poll loop exits on
   SDXC_INTERRUPT_ERROR_BIT, which contains the data-side bits --
   DATA_CRC_ERROR, DATA_TIMEOUT, FIFO_RUN_ERROR, START_BIT_ERROR,
   END_BIT_ERROR. Those belong to the transfer being aborted, and with the
   card still driving an unread FIFO they keep latching. At a 200 MHz HS400
   clock the 1 KiB FIFO refills in a few microseconds, which is well inside
   the wakeup latency of the threaded handler, so the first read of RINTSTS
   can already carry an overflow error and the loop ends before the CIU has
   run the command. The "failed" line is then printed for a stop that was
   never judged.

3. No recovery. Whatever the stop did, nothing resets the controller
   afterwards, which is what turns one failed stop into a host that is
   finished until the next power cycle.

WHERE THE VENDOR DRIVER DIFFERS, AND WHAT THIS PATCH DOES
---------------------------------------------------------
Read from the A733 BSP's sunxi-mmc.c (the SoC ops in sunxi-mmc-v4p5x.c carry
nothing stop-related; the stop path is entirely in the vendor core).

Taken over: its poll loop calls cond_resched(). Ours spun a CPU for
up to a second in a threaded IRQ; this patch yields the same way and says so at
the call site.

Not taken over:

  - Same ordering defect. Its sunxi_mmc_finalize_request() does the identical
    teardown -- REG_IDST 0x337, IDMAC soft reset, DMA_RESET, DMA_ENB cleared,
    FIFO_RESET -- in the interrupt path, before the stop. It does not get the
    order right; it survives it by bracketing the stop with two full controller
    resets (sunxi_mmc_clk_gating_reset(), i.e. regs_save, bus clock and reset
    line off and on again, regs_restore) plus a clock update on each side. That
    recovers from the ordering instead of avoiding it, and it costs a save/restore
    of ten registers plus the SoC-specific set on every data error.

  - Same verdict defect. Its sunxi_mmc_send_manual_stop() polls on the same
    full SDXC_INTERRUPT_ERROR_BIT mask and never clears SMHC_RINTSTS before
    issuing the command. Its reset-before-stop clears the register as a side
    effect.

  - It suppresses the failure message. The "send manual stop command failed" line is
    suppressed whenever host->execute_tuning_runing is set -- exactly the case
    that produces failed stops most often. This tree keeps the line and adds
    the register state to it; diagnostic lines in this tree are never
    removed, only lowered in visibility once the hardware has proven a fix.

  - Its barrier comment does not match its code: the comment asks for the
    command before the argument, while the wmb() sits after the arg write and
    before the command write. Not copied. Two writel()s to the same device are ordered on arm64.

  - Its watchdog (sunxi_timerout_work, 5 s, gated on
    SUNXI_SC_EN_TIMEOUT_DETECT) is not a blueprint for the hang either: for a
    read whose command has already completed its handler re-arms and does
    nothing.

WHAT MAINLINE DOES
------------------
dw_mmc, from which this controller family descends, sends the abort first and
stops the DMA second, at all three of its data-error sites:

    send_stop_abort(host, data);
    dw_mci_stop_dma(host);

(drivers/mmc/host/dw_mmc.c, STATE_SENDING_CMD / STATE_SENDING_DATA /
STATE_DATA_BUSY). In STATE_SENDING_STOP it then calls dw_mci_reset() when the
data command errored -- CTRL_RESET | FIFO_RESET | DMA_RESET, followed by
SDMMC_CMD_UPD_CLK, because a controller reset costs the CIU its clock.

mtk-sd calls msdc_reset_hw() -- MSDC_CFG_RST plus a FIFO clear -- on every data
error.

Both of those are what this patch does. Neither of them has our second defect,
because neither judges a stop by polling a shared raw status register.

WHAT THIS CHANGES
-----------------
- sunxi_mmc_release_data_engine() is the old teardown, unchanged in content,
  factored out of sunxi_mmc_finalize_request(). Its comment says what DMA_ENB
  actually does, so the next reader does not mistake it for a way to stop a
  transfer.

- finalize defers it when the request still owes the card a stop
  (host->manual_stop_mrq set). The threaded handler sends the stop first, then
  releases the data engine. Nothing changes for a request that needs no stop,
  which is every successful one.

- sunxi_mmc_send_manual_stop() clears SMHC_RINTSTS immediately before the
  command and judges the result on SDXC_STOP_ERROR_BITS -- RESP_ERROR,
  RESP_CRC_ERROR, RESP_TIMEOUT, HARD_WARE_LOCKED. HARD_WARE_LOCKED stays in on
  purpose: that is the CIU refusing to load the command, which is this
  command's own failure. It returns whether the controller reported
  COMMAND_DONE, and on failure logs RINTSTS, SMHC_STATUS and SMHC_CMD, so the
  three cases -- command never run, command refused, card did not answer --
  are distinguishable in a log instead of sharing one sentence.

- sunxi_mmc_reset_after_failed_stop() runs only when the stop failed: SOFT_RST
  | FIFO_RST | DMA_RST as a read-modify-write on SMHC_CTRL, the register
  written back afterwards, everything sunxi_mmc_init_host() programs after its
  own reset programmed again, and a clock update. The read-modify-write is
  deliberate -- sunxi_mmc_reset_host() writes SMHC_CTRL whole and is only safe
  at init. The re-programming is deliberate too: the UM does not list what
  SOFT_RST clears, but it does say that SMHC_EXT_CMD's AUTO_CMD23_EN (11.3.8.34)
  is cleared by SOFT_RST, so nothing in the register
  file is assumed to have survived. That bit in particular is already re-armed
  per request by `mmc-sunxi-a733-emmc-hardware-auto-cmd23`, which is why a reset here does not cost auto-CMD23.

- sunxi_mmc_program_host() is the second half of sunxi_mmc_init_host(), split
  out so the recovery can call it. No behaviour change at init.

THESIS
------
The stop fails because the data path is taken apart before the card is told to
stop, and because the stop's verdict is read from a status register that the
still-running transfer keeps writing into. Stop first, judge the stop by its
own events, and reset the controller when it still does not take.

FALSIFIER
---------
On the 8 GiB board, with hs400_ds_dl = 21 and the same sustained O_DIRECT read:

  - PASS: the log shows "data error, sending stop command" and no "send stop
    command failed"; the read ends in an I/O error instead of D state; a second
    sustained read at hs400_ds_dl = 11 immediately afterwards runs at the
    normal ~139 MiB/s without a power cycle.

  - REFUTED, and the order was not the cause: "send stop command failed" still
    appears. Then read the new fields on that line. rint carrying
    HARD_WARE_LOCKED means the CIU refuses the command while the data FSM is
    busy, and a stop cannot be the first step -- the reset has to come first,
    which is what the vendor does and would then be right for a reason we did
    not have. rint with no bit at all and cmdr with START still set means the
    command was never loaded, same conclusion. A response error means the card
    ignored a CMD12 it should honour, and the question moves to the card.

  - PARTIALLY REFUTED: the stop succeeds but the device is still unusable
    afterwards. Then SMHC_STATUS FSM_BUSY after the stop is the observable, and
    sunxi_mmc_reset_after_failed_stop() has to run unconditionally rather than
    only on failure.

This patch does not claim to fix the absence of a data timeout. A transfer
that stalls and raises no error at all never reaches this path; that is a
separate defect and a separate patch. What it claims is that once an error IS
raised, the recovery works instead of wedging the host.

VERIFIED / NOT VERIFIED
-----------------------
- Applies clean at fuzz 0 on a fresh unpack of 6.18.36 with the full series in
  numeric order: yes, 190/190 (local, 2026-09-20).
- Builds: NOT claimed here -- the compile gate is the automated build job, not this header.
- Silicon: NOT proven. The falsifier above is the whole hardware claim, and it
  is one run on the 8 GiB board with the sysfs knob from `mmc-sunxi-a-landed-knob-for-the-hs400-data-strobe-delay`.
- Not exercised by any other path in this tree today: the manual stop only runs
  on a data error, and the only reproducible data error we have is the strobe
  sweep's own failures and the hs400_ds_dl = 21 case.

ISSUE
-----
 Symptom it is meant to remove: the permanently hung read described above.

